{"connection_id": "BC-ea5f2ca8faac7e623d1c", "ref": "Didache 1:1", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways of life and death", "note": "The Didache opens with two ways, one of life and one of death. Its explicit road-formulation belongs beside the commentary's one route whose ends become ease or hardship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4201d849f7a5a174e0aa", "ref": "Epistle of Barnabas 18:1", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way of light and way of darkness", "note": "Barnabas introduces two ways of teaching and authority, light and darkness. This Christian two-way schema sharpens the commentary's moral split between following guidance and moving away from warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7281e7157644b0677800", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flee idolatry", "note": "Paul's direct command to flee idolatry supplies a Christian imperative for the commentary's inverted use of janb: deliberate distance from the false object rather than distance from divine reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a85228e30fd9479e490a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.5.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "commandments not burdensome", "note": "First John says love of God keeps his commandments and that those commandments are not burdensome. It provides a concise Christian analogue to the commentary's ease as obedient responsiveness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ed2d3045fcaeee62e24a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all flesh as withering grass", "note": "First Peter repeats that all flesh is grass and that grass withers. It gives the commentary's pasture-to-waste image a Christian scriptural reception centered on mortality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2aee38f0e68c96d0d6d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "following in Christ's steps", "note": "Christ leaves an example so believers may follow in his steps. This is a Christian ethical use of the commentary's following-behind-a-guide image, without its animal figure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-62f70c18a7224c3bbbf1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straying sheep returned to shepherd", "note": "People once strayed like sheep but have returned to the shepherd and overseer of their souls. It gives a Christian recovery-from-wrong-route counterpart to the commentary's guide and its animal family."}
{"connection_id": "BC-172c872cf1c6775764eb", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.1.9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turning from idols to God", "note": "The Thessalonians turn to God from idols to serve the living and true God. That conversion gives a New Testament counterpart to Abraham's desire that he and his sons be kept from idol worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7350c7aacd82b70bdd65", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.6.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "temple of God versus idols", "note": "Paul contrasts the temple of the living God with idols. This establishes the Christian side of the commentary's Abrahamic separation between divine worship and false objects."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f4ccddbf5830e5b6023", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.6.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "come out and be separate", "note": "The command to come out from their midst and be separate gives bodily force to the commentary's janb image of keeping oneself away from a thing; its immediate setting is the idol contrast of 6:16."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f79e9feb992602482f05", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Balaam as one who leaves the straight way", "note": "The commentary says a traveler can take a wrong course while the road remains; 2 Peter interprets Balaam as one who leaves the straight way for wrongdoing, giving the Numbers donkey episode an explicit moral reading of straying for gain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-462be99a21bf46bd7fb3", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Balaam as one leaving the straight way", "note": "Second Peter calls corrupt teachers people who leave the straight way and follow Balaam's way. Read beside Numbers 22:23, it turns the donkey-and-road episode into a Christian moral warning relevant to the commentary's guidance and wrong turning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92a135e90372dcc00ece", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.14.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turn from futile things to the living Creator", "note": "Paul and Barnabas urge idolizing crowds to turn from vain things to the living God who made heaven and earth. It directly connects the commentary's creation claim to its movement away from idolatry."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f158987c5f7074254f16", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.15.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "unbearable yoke", "note": "Acts calls a disputed yoke one that neither ancestors nor present hearers could bear. In contrast to the commentary's mount readily yielding to its rider, this verse tests which religious burden is actually bearable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd5d5bdf6a2d9c6693bc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.22.11", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blind traveler led by hand", "note": "After the heavenly encounter leaves Saul unable to see, his companions lead him by hand to Damascus. It offers a concrete Christian counterpart to the commentary's weakened traveler needing bodily guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d6e3317fc5dd7d0c706", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a man lame from birth is carried to the temple gate", "note": "The commentary’s secondary walking image is a weak person who relies on support; Acts describes a man lame from birth being carried and placed at the temple gate, a specific scene of dependence before his healing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b7750c76f07deee348e6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a lame man is raised by the hand and strengthened", "note": "The commentary recalls a weak person who needs support to walk; Peter takes the man lame from birth by the hand and raises him, and his feet and ankles are strengthened, a concrete healing of unsupported gait."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0510e5a02909bfe311f0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.8", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the healed man walks and leaps", "note": "The commentary’s lexical image is a weak, swaying walker; after the healing in Acts, the man enters the temple walking, leaping, and praising God, showing the restored movement that support makes possible."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8f30c44eb309e43e449", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.2.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "created for works prepared to walk in", "note": "Believers are called God's workmanship, created for works God prepared beforehand so that they may walk in them. It combines the commentary's creation language with a divinely prepared course of action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef92c9a9859fdc249add", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bearing one another's burdens", "note": "The commentary's weak walker is sustained by others and its easy route relieves hardship. Paul commands mutual burden-bearing, an ethical counterpart without the travel scene itself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ccf709989f8caebe8a7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weak hands and feeble knees restored", "note": "Hebrews calls for drooping hands and weak knees to be strengthened. Together with its next verse on straight paths, it supplies the bodily side of the commentary's supported traveler."}
{"connection_id": "BC-36e1adf59b7748519c3b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight paths for the feet so the lame may be healed", "note": "The commentary pairs a difficult ascent with weak, swaying walking; Hebrews commands straight paths for the feet so the lame limb will be healed rather than injured, joining path preparation to bodily weakness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-67311f675df105c2df36", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight paths for lame feet", "note": "The commentary's route concerns weak movement and a path made easy. Hebrews orders straight paths for the feet so that what is lame is healed rather than put out of joint."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf6843b8ad90abb2ff12", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.15", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing and hardened hearts", "note": "Hebrews repeats the summons to hear God's voice today and not harden the heart. It places the commentary's contrast between fruitful reminder and wretched avoidance in an exhortatory Christian reception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e6799275b525546f4eb3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.8", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unfruitful ground ending in burning", "note": "Land bearing thorns and thistles is rejected, near a curse, and ends in burning. This extends the commentary's vegetation-and-fire sequence through a Christian agricultural warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-51cbedbab7bc7cbcaaad", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers and flower falls", "note": "James describes the sun's scorching rise: grass withers, its flower falls, and its beauty perishes. This supplies a Christian moral use of the transience visually developed in the commentary's pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f74f1cff86a08f33f8fe", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.3.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "horse made obedient", "note": "James puts bits in horses' mouths so that they obey and their whole bodies are guided. Its explicit horse, obedience, and direction closely illuminate the commentary's easy-to-lead mount."}
{"connection_id": "BC-43877752ee23e9cfa112", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "John identifies himself as Isaiah’s wilderness voice", "note": "The commentary develops the guide and the way that is made ready; John applies Isaiah’s road-preparation oracle to his own role as herald, reading the route as preparation of people for the Lord’s coming."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4ddd039f97c9b2a3259", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shepherd before sheep", "note": "The commentary's hadi goes ahead and the animal follows readily. Jesus' shepherd goes before his sheep, and they follow because they know his voice, a close Christian pastoral rendering of both actions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-660566054e93bd2664c2", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "sheep flee a stranger", "note": "The commentary treats turning aside from the true reminder as the wretched response. Jesus' sheep flee a stranger precisely because they do not know that voice, showing that withdrawal is right or wrong according to the guide recognized."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b031fac5a214f4c0cc57", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.6", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus as the way", "note": "Jesus identifies himself as the way leading to the Father. This is a concentrated Christian transformation of the commentary's guide, path, and destination pattern."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4b01e2021ec5c88f29b0", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.16.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Spirit guiding into truth", "note": "The Spirit is said to guide the disciples into all truth. It transfers the commentary's guide who shows the road into a Christian account of revelatory direction, rather than treating guidance as mere locomotion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d57589d7aa8172f4252", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.66", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "disciples withdraw and stop walking with Jesus", "note": "After Jesus' difficult teaching, many disciples go back and no longer walk with him. It provides a Christian scene of abandoning a guide after hearing his words, close to the commentary's wretched avoidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0913cb74cd261ee3d132", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.8.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "following the light without walking in darkness", "note": "Jesus says that whoever follows him will not walk in darkness but have the light of life. It makes following the true guide the opposite of the commentary's lost or warning-avoiding traveler."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2844af14010ec920c836", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.79", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide our feet into the way of peace", "note": "The commentary develops guidance as showing a road and easing the traveler’s way; Zechariah’s prophecy says the dawn from on high will guide people’s feet into the way of peace, directly joining guidance, feet, and route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-31c9233afc30e89edf04", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.79", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feet guided into peace", "note": "Zechariah's prophecy says the divine visitation will guide feet into the way of peace. It turns the commentary's road-leading and calm gait into an explicitly salvific destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3e78fb727737f8aa754", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.34", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wounded man on an animal", "note": "The commentary turns the promise of ease into a rider-and-mount picture. The Samaritan places the wounded traveler on his own animal and conveys him to care, making the animal an instrument of supported passage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-90c57dbc0d0c935224ca", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one side of the afterlife sees the other", "note": "In the commentary’s cited scene a person in bliss looks down and sees his former companion in Hell; Luke has the tormented rich man lift his eyes and see Lazarus with Abraham at a distance, reversing the direction of the postmortem gaze."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d6093c94e6d9008195c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "tormented man sees the blessed afar", "note": "The commentary's wrong road culminates with a blessed person looking down to a companion in hell's center. Luke reverses the viewpoint: a tormented rich man sees Abraham and Lazarus far away, a related but differently arranged afterlife sightline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad0d5d102449fd0f1d6c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prepare the Lord's way", "note": "Luke quotes the call to prepare the Lord's way and make his paths straight. It is the Christian continuation that supplies the guide-and-road formula distinct from the terrain changes in Luke 3:5."}

