{"connection_id": "BC-af8fe79255c1801cafdf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountains low and roads smooth", "note": "Luke applies the leveling of valleys, mountains, crooked places, and rough roads to the coming salvation. It gives a Christian reception of the terrain that the commentary reads as a journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ebb9352d7fdf3a00183", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word beside the road", "note": "In Luke's explanation, seed beside the road is the word heard and then removed before belief. It gives the commentary's image of keeping the admonition at one's roadside an explicit parabolic setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a5815a324f4d3a684539", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.10.32", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus walking ahead toward Jerusalem", "note": "On the road to Jerusalem, Jesus goes ahead of the disciples while they follow in awe and fear. The scene realizes the commentary's guide who moves in front of those on the journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1bd2f5b8498264225268", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.28", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weary and burdened people receive rest", "note": "The commentary reads the promise of ease as relief on a journey; Jesus invites the weary and burdened to come to him and promises rest, giving the ease image a human burden-bearing context."}
{"connection_id": "BC-58e1efecfa7f49ba2c04", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.28", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weary and burdened people invited to rest", "note": "Before calling his yoke easy, Jesus invites those laboring and heavily burdened to come for rest. This names the human hardship to which the commentary's promise of ease responds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-abce7d5cdbcb74a162f2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.29", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "take the yoke, learn, and find rest", "note": "The commentary’s easy mount follows its guide and carries its rider along the way; Jesus invites hearers to take his yoke and learn from him, promising rest for their souls, a distinct image of following under an easy discipline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4dce361bb33d1fc48cd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.30", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "easy yoke and light burden", "note": "The commentary’s mount is readily led and takes the way with light steps; Jesus calls his yoke easy and his burden light, applying ease to the discipline of following him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa12d330736f602ce5c5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.30", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "easy yoke and light burden", "note": "The commentary's yusr is ease, compliant following, and light movement. Jesus calls his yoke easy and burden light, a direct Christian moral use of ease in animal-yoke imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7b9f88e79b5ab48c759", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.21.7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "garments laid on a mount for Jesus to sit", "note": "The commentary develops a cover placed on a camel's hump and a rider settling on the animal. Matthew narrates garments laid on the donkey and colt before Jesus sits, a literal Christian journey image of mount and seat-covering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8872d5a15913093cf0de", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.3.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chaff burned in unquenchable fire", "note": "The commentary places ghutha beside the wretched person's great fire. Matthew's winnowing scene gathers chaff and burns it, giving an eschatological continuation to that agricultural residue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-232bc889ce7229d9415f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two gates and roads with different ends", "note": "The commentary sets a shown route beside a steep climb that is not attempted; Jesus describes a broad road entered by a wide gate that leads to destruction, making the traveler’s chosen way consequential."}
{"connection_id": "BC-77988b26e09bdb5394db", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "broad way ending in destruction", "note": "Matthew contrasts the broad way that leads to destruction with the life-giving entrance. It gives a concrete New Testament road division for the commentary's opposed courses of ease and hardship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50e7f337ec759c674e79", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "narrow way leading to life", "note": "The commentary’s two high routes and the unattempted ascent invite comparison with Jesus’ narrow way to life, which few find; this adds a sharply difficult route alongside the easy-way image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78e4441d8b5923d1fac2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "constricted way leading to life", "note": "The commentary can picture the difficult side as the road of wretchedness, while Matthew calls the life-giving way constricted and hard. The contrary assignment of difficulty makes the comparison interpretively useful rather than a simple equation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20d24d9edf33b7285d74", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Lamb shepherding and guiding to waters", "note": "The Lamb shepherds the redeemed and guides them to springs of living water. It reverses ordinary rider-and-animal roles while retaining the commentary's leading figure and safe destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7253950db8301235e756", "ref": "1QS 3:17–18", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two spirits for human walking", "note": "The Two Spirits Treatise says that God made humanity and appointed two spirits for people to walk by until visitation. It is an unusually close Second Temple analogue to the commentary's created traveler facing divergent roads."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1f5a677193f36c30a5a", "ref": "4 Ezra 7:6–9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "narrow path between fire and water", "note": "4 Ezra imagines the entrance to a city as a single narrow path between fire and deep water. Its perilous route makes the commentary's ridge, choice, and final destination into a vivid Jewish apocalyptic travel scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-905b0f047034ba55896a", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Makkot 10b", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "one is led on the path one chooses", "note": "The Talmud states that a person is led on the way the person wishes to go. This rabbinic maxim is especially close to the commentary's claim that the same divine easing works toward ease or hardship according to the traveler's direction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd775dd9719c546f39a4", "ref": "Baruch 5:7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leveled return road", "note": "Baruch says God orders mountains lowered and valleys filled so Israel can walk safely. Its return procession makes the commentary's high route and divinely eased walking a single scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f86402d3dd652420114b", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 38:13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham breaks his father’s idols", "note": "The commentary recalls Abraham’s prayer that he and his descendants be kept from idol worship; Genesis Rabbah’s story of the young Abraham breaking the idols in Terah’s shop interprets that separation as an active rejection of his father’s cult."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc9401f0a8fe02c914aa", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 38:13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham destroys Terah's idols", "note": "This midrash expands Abraham's anti-idolatry into the episode of smashing Terah's images. It gives Jewish narrative force to the commentary's Abrahamic request for separation from idols, while remaining an interpretive tradition rather than a shared biblical scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ef8047446f7058fe55c", "ref": "Jubilees 12:12–14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham burns the idol house", "note": "Jubilees portrays Abram burning the house of idols and the resulting crisis in Terah's household. The expansion makes active rupture with idols the narrative counterpart to the commentary's Abrahamic prayer for protection from them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc65429286c02f5c5330", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "yoke of Torah and released burdens", "note": "Avot says accepting the yoke of Torah removes other yokes, while throwing it off brings them on. This rabbinic interpretation makes the commentary's ease and hardship consequences of what one willingly follows."}
{"connection_id": "BC-49728b4e285f1c20ad3e", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 5:20", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "be swift as a deer and light as an eagle", "note": "The commentary’s lexicon gives a mount that follows quickly and has light legs; Rabbi Judah ben Tema uses deer-swiftness and eagle-lightness as traits for doing the will of Heaven, turning animal agility into an ethical image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d0d36ad115fbbb3beed", "ref": "Sirach 15:17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life and death are set before a person to choose", "note": "The commentary links guidance with a traveler’s response and outcome; Sirach says life and death are before a person and the choice is given to him, extending the choice-of-way motif into Jewish wisdom teaching."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6831fd8628dedf554e2e", "ref": "Sirach 15:17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life and death set before a person", "note": "Sirach sets life and death before human beings and says each receives what is chosen. It provides a wisdom-text counterpart to the commentary's guided road that divides according to the traveler's response."}
